<header><p class="st-kicker">county · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Stolberg-Wernigerode</h1><p class="st-meta">1645 CE – 1806 CE · capital Wernigerode</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Harz county of the Stolberg family, a centre of German Pietism dominated from its castle above Wernigerode.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-stolberg">Stolberg</a> acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wernigerode">Wernigerode</a> in 1429. Repeated divisions of the family lands produced a separate line of Stolberg-Wernigerode in 1645, ruling the town and the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brocken">Brocken</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The county had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and iron mining and forestry in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a>. In 1714 a treaty acknowledged the overlordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, limiting the counts' independence. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-ernst-count-of-stolberg-wernigerode">Christian Ernst</a>, from 1710 to 1771, the court became a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietism">Pietism</a>, with hymn books, a fine library and links to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/halle-saale">Halle</a>, and the counts supported church and school reforms.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The county was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-westphalia">Kingdom of Westphalia</a> in 1807 and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815, while the family kept its estates. Its counts were later raised to princes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-prince-of-stolberg-wernigerode">Otto of Stolberg-Wernigerode</a> served as Prussian vice-chancellor under Bismarck.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1645 CE – 1806 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.44%;width:3.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1645 CE – 1806 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wernigerode</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Christian Ernst, Henry Ernest</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Stolberg-Wernigerode</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1726&amp;focus=county-of-stolberg-wernigerode" aria-label="County of Stolberg-Wernigerode on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4923 385 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County of Stolberg-Wernigerode lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5243 641L5142 672M5243 641L5229 694M5243 641L5300 622M5243 641L5215 656"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5142 672h0M5229 694h0M5300 622h0M5215 656h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5243 641h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5243 641h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Wernigerode. <a href="/atlas?year=1726&amp;focus=county-of-stolberg-wernigerode">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
